<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arduin Glaber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 977</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arduin Glaber was a 10th-century count who received control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and the Susa valley around 941 and founded the March of Turin. He drove the Saracens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> from the Alpine passes. His great-granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a> brought his lands to the house of Savoy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arduin-glaber.jpg" alt="Arduin Glaber" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marchese Arduino · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardouin_(comte_de_Turin).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arduin, called Glaber or il Glabro, the Bald, was the eldest son of Roger, Count of Auriate, a Frankish nobleman who had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> early in the 10th century. Auriate was the region bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, the Po River and the Stura, today the Saluzzese and Cuneese. Arduin succeeded his father there around 935, but he is first documented as count on 13 April 945, sitting in judgement at a placitum of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranc">Lanfranc</a> at Pavia before King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. The chronicle of the abbey of Novalesa, the Chronicon Novaliciense, is the main source for his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his campaign in the Susa Valley he brought Novalesa back under Christian control and built the first castle at Avigliana. King Hugh had exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy">Berengar of Ivrea</a> and abolished the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a>, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, in 941, and probably gave Arduin the county then; Turin became the main residence of his dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a>. He stayed on good terms with Berengar, who became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 15 December 950, and on 13 November 950 he received the administration of Novalesa. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> invaded Italy, Arduin changed sides during the siege of Canossa, and Otto later made him count of Asti.</p><p>Arduin is first recorded as margrave in 964, so Otto may have created the title for him. His march comprised Auriate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, Asti, Albenga and probably Bredulo, Alba and Ventimiglia, some of them likely taken by conquest. He took part in the wars of William I and Rotbold I of Provence against the Saracens, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-of-cremona">Liutprand of Cremona</a> he and Rotbold led the successful assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> itself in 972 or 973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arduin was last recorded alive on 4 April 976. Although he had recovered their lands, the monks of Novalesa accused him of disregarding their rights and described him as a powerful man full of vices, swollen with pride and greedy for the goods of others. He married a woman recorded as Vmille, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a> or Immula, and had at least five children. His son and successor Manfred I married Prangarda, daughter of Adalbert Atto of Canossa, and his daughters Alsinda and Richilda married Giselbert II of Bergamo and Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arduin Glaber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 941 King Hugh of Arles or his successor reorganised the western Alps and Piedmont, giving <b>Arduin Glaber</b> control of Turin and the Susa valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-turin">March of Turin</a></li></ul></section>
